---
title: 'Batch Editing'
enterprise: true
---

Batch editing allows you to edit multiple cells or rows in the grid before committing or reverting these edits. This is useful for scenarios where you want to make several edits at once without immediately updating the data source.

{% note %}
Batch editing is an advanced feature only available via the API to allow you to tailor it to your specific needs.
{% /note %}

## Enabling Batch Editing

Batch Editing is enabled by calling the grid API method `startBatchEdit()`.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    onGridReady: (params) => {
        params.api.startBatchEdit();
    }
}
```

Once enabled, `api.commitBatchEdit()` or `api.cancelBatchEdit()` can be called to complete the batch edit operation.

{% note %}
Batch Edit is only compatible with the [Client-Side Row Model](./row-models/).
{% /note %}

## Batch Editing API

Using the following API calls, you can build your own experience around AG Grid's batch editing functionality.

The following example demonstrates a simple batch editing scenario: starting a batch, queuing edits and then either committing them or discarding them.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Batch Editing API" name="batch-editing-api" exampleHeight=560 /%}

The grid API methods `startEditingCell()` and `stopEditing()` can be used to control the editing state of individual cells. When you start editing a cell after enabling Batch Editing, the grid enters a batch editing mode where it can track changes. `isBatchEditing()` can be used to check if the grid is currently in batch editing mode. In this mode, `startEditingCell()` and `stopEditing()` affect only the cell editors, not the overall batch.

{% apiDocumentation source="grid-api/api.json" section="batch-editing" names=["isBatchEditing", "startBatchEdit", "commitBatchEdit", "cancelBatchEdit"] /%}

## Full Row Batch Editing

[Full Row](./cell-editing-full-row/) is supported in Batch Editing, adding each cell's pending value to the batch.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Batch Editing FullRow" name="batch-editing-fullrow" exampleHeight=560 /%}

## Batch Editing Lifecycle

Cell and row editing events (`cellEditingStarted`, `cellEditingStopped`, etc.) fire normally when cell editors open and close, just as in default editing.

The key difference in batch mode is that **`cellValueChanged` and `rowValueChanged` are deferred** — they fire only when `commitBatchEdit()` is called, not when the user finishes editing a single cell or when `setDataValue` stages a value. If `cancelBatchEdit()` is called instead, pending values are silently discarded and no value-changed events fire.

### Pending Values

When Batch Editing is active, edits made to cells are stored as **pending values**. These pending values are not immediately applied to the underlying data model. Instead, they are held in a temporary state until the batch is either committed or cancelled.

- **Display features** (cell rendering, copy/paste) use pending values so the user sees their edits reflected in the grid immediately.
- **Data features** (sorting, filtering, grouping, aggregation) continue to use the committed data and are only updated after the batch is committed.
- **On cancel**, all pending values are discarded, the grid reverts to the committed data, and no `cellValueChanged` events fire.

### Reading Values During a Batch

Two APIs can read cell values at different stages of the batch lifecycle. They differ in **default behaviour** and **purpose**:

- **`api.getCellValue()`** — returns the **display value** (defaults to `from: 'edit'`, showing pending edits). Use for UI-facing reads.
- **`rowNode.getDataValue()`** — returns the **data value** (defaults to `from: 'data'`, ignoring pending edits). Use for data-facing reads.

**`api.getCellValue({ rowNode, colKey, from? })`**

| `from` | Returns |
|---|---|
| `'edit'` (default) | Live editor typing → pending batch value → committed data |
| `'batch'` | Pending batch value → committed data (excludes live editor typing) |
| `'data'` | Committed data only, ignoring all edit state |

{% apiDocumentation source="grid-api/api.json" section="miscellaneous" names=["getCellValue"] /%}

**`rowNode.getDataValue(colKey, from?)`**

| `from` | Returns |
|---|---|
| `'data'` (default) | Committed data only, ignoring all edit state |
| `'batch'` | Pending batch value → committed data (excludes live editor typing) |
| `'edit'` | Live editor typing → pending batch value → committed data |

```js
api.startBatchEdit();

// Stage a pending edit
rowNode.setDataValue('price', 99, 'batch');

// Read the value at each stage
rowNode.getDataValue('price');            // 42 (committed — batch not yet applied)
rowNode.getDataValue('price', 'batch');  // 99 (staged pending value)
rowNode.getDataValue('price', 'edit');   // 99 (no editor open, so same as 'batch')

api.commitBatchEdit();

rowNode.getDataValue('price');            // 99 (now committed to data)
```

`rowNode.getDataValue()` also supports `'value'` and `'data-raw'` modes for working with aggregation and raw data — see [Advanced getDataValue Modes](#advanced-getdatavalue-modes) below.

### Programmatic Value Updates with setDataValue

`rowNode.setDataValue(colKey, newValue, eventSource?)` writes a value to a row node programmatically. The optional `eventSource` parameter controls exactly how the value is applied:

| `eventSource` | Batch mode active | Batch mode inactive |
| ------------- | --- | --- |
| (default) | Stages a pending batch edit. Any open cell editor is **closed**. | If that cell's editor is open, it is closed and the value is written to data. Otherwise writes directly to data. |
| `'edit'` | If an editor is open, updates the live editor value without committing. If the editor doesn't support `refresh()`, the grid may recreate it and restore focus. Otherwise stages a pending batch edit. | If an editor is open, updates the live editor value without committing. If the editor doesn't support `refresh()`, the grid may recreate it and restore focus. Otherwise writes directly to data. |
| `'batch'` | Stages a pending batch edit. Any open cell editor is **closed**. | Writes directly to data. Any open editor on that cell stays open (editor value is not affected). |
| `'data'` | Writes directly to data, **bypassing batch mode entirely**. The value is not staged — it skips pending state and is immediately committed to the row's data. | Writes directly to data. |

The `'edit'` source mirrors `getDataValue(col, 'edit')`: it targets the live editor value. The grid updates the editor value in the edit model and calls `editor.refresh(params)` when available. Editors that don't implement `refresh()` are recreated to pick up the value while preserving focus.

**Without batch mode**, all sources write directly to the row's data:

```js
// No batch active — writes immediately to data
rowNode.setDataValue('price', 150);
rowNode.getDataValue('price');          // 150
```

**With `'edit'`** — updates an open editor without closing it:

```js
api.startBatchEdit();
api.startEditingCell({ rowIndex: 0, colKey: 'price' });

// Push a value into the open editor
rowNode.setDataValue('price', 99, 'edit');

// The editor value is updated (custom editors see this via refresh())
api.getCellValue({ rowNode, colKey: 'price', from: 'edit' }); // 99
rowNode.data.price;                                             // 42 (data unchanged)

api.stopEditing();  // stages the editor value as a pending batch edit
api.commitBatchEdit();

rowNode.getDataValue('price');  // 99 (now committed to data)
```

**With `'batch'`** — stages pending edits across multiple cells:

```js
api.startBatchEdit();

rowNode.setDataValue('price', 99, 'batch');
rowNode.setDataValue('qty', 5, 'batch');

// Data is unchanged; pending values are staged
rowNode.data.price;                      // 42 (original data)
rowNode.getDataValue('price');           // 42 (committed data, batch not yet applied)
rowNode.getDataValue('price', 'batch'); // 99 (staged pending value)

api.commitBatchEdit();

rowNode.getDataValue('price');           // 99 (now committed to data)
```

**With `'data'`** — writes directly to data at any time, bypassing batch state entirely:

```js
api.startBatchEdit();

rowNode.setDataValue('price', 99, 'data');

// Written immediately — no pending edit, data is already updated
rowNode.data.price;                      // 99 (already in data)
rowNode.getDataValue('price');           // 99
rowNode.getDataValue('price', 'batch'); // 99 (no staged edit; 'batch' falls back to committed)

api.cancelBatchEdit(); // cancels any other pending edits, but 'data' write is permanent
```

### Advanced getDataValue Modes

In addition to `'data'`, `'batch'`, and `'edit'`, `rowNode.getDataValue()` supports two more `from` modes for working with aggregation and raw data:

-   **`'value'`**: Same as `'data'` but unwraps aggregation objects to plain scalars. The built-in `avg` and `count` functions return wrapper objects — `'value'` extracts the numeric result (via `toNumber()` or `.value`). For `sum`, `min`, `max`, `first`, `last`, and most custom agg functions, `'data'` and `'value'` are identical.
-   **`'data-raw'`**: Reads directly from `rowNode.data`, bypassing aggregation, valueGetters, and formula resolution. On group rows this returns the group node's own data (if any), not the aggregated result. On formula cells, returns the raw formula string.

#### Aggregation Wrappers (`avg` and `count`)

The built-in `avg` and `count` aggregation functions return an `IAggFuncResult` wrapper object so that the grid can show both the value and a count:

-   `avg` → `{ value: number | bigint | null, count: number, toString(), toNumber() }`
-   `count` → `{ value: number, toString(), toNumber() }`

Other built-in functions (`sum`, `min`, `max`, `first`, `last`) and most custom agg functions return plain scalars, so `'data'` and `'value'` are identical for them.

```js
// Column 'revenue' uses aggFunc: 'avg'.
// Leaf rows have { revenue: 420 }, { revenue: 380 }, { revenue: 460 } — avg = 420.

// 'data' (default) — returns the raw IAggFuncResult object
const result = groupRowNode.getDataValue('revenue');          // { value: 420, count: 3, toString(), toNumber() }
result.value;                                                 // 420
result.count;                                                 // 3
String(result);                                               // '420' (via toString())

// 'value' — unwraps IAggFuncResult to its .value property
const avg = groupRowNode.getDataValue('revenue', 'value');    // 420

// 'data-raw' — reads the raw 'revenue' field on the group node's own data object,
// bypassing aggregation entirely. Group rows do not own leaf data, so this is undefined.
const raw = groupRowNode.getDataValue('revenue', 'data-raw'); // undefined
```

For a column using `aggFunc: 'count'`:

```js
// 'data' — IAggFuncResult wrapper
const countResult = groupRowNode.getDataValue('revenue', 'data'); // { value: 3, toString(), toNumber() }

// 'value' — unwrapped scalar
const count = groupRowNode.getDataValue('revenue', 'value');      // 3
```

For a column using a custom agg function that returns a plain number, `'data'` and `'value'` are the same:

```js
const colDef = { field: 'revenue', aggFunc: params => params.values.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) };

// Custom agg returns a plain number — 'data' and 'value' are identical
groupRowNode.getDataValue('revenue', 'data');  // 1260
groupRowNode.getDataValue('revenue', 'value'); // 1260
```

#### `'data-raw'` vs valueGetters

For leaf rows, `'data'` and `'data-raw'` differ when the column has a valueGetter:

```js
// Column 'total' has: valueGetter: p => p.data.price * p.data.qty

// 'data' / 'value' — runs the valueGetter
const total    = rowNode.getDataValue('total', 'value');     // 600 (100 * 6)

// 'data-raw' — reads the raw 'total' field directly, skipping the valueGetter
const rawTotal = rowNode.getDataValue('total', 'data-raw');  // undefined (no 'total' field exists)
```

## Customisation

### Custom Renderers & Editors

The `ICellRendererComp` interface provides a means for your cell renderer to update when edits are batched, to reflect the pending nature of the values. The `refresh` method that the grid calls when the cell needs to be re-rendered. The parameters include the latest pending value.

{% interfaceDocumentation interfaceName="ICellRendererComp" names=["refresh"] /%}

As with custom renderers, the `ICellEditor` interface includes the `refresh` method, which the grid calls when the cell needs to be re-rendered. The parameters include the latest pending value.

{% interfaceDocumentation interfaceName="ICellEditor" names=["refresh"] /%}

{% gridExampleRunner title="Batch Editing Customization" name="batch-editing-custom" exampleHeight=560 /%}

### Styling

Pending edit styles can be overridden using css, via the `.ag-cell-edit-color` and `.ag-row-batch-edit` classes.

```scss
.ag-cell-batch-edit {
    background-color: var(--ag-cell-batch-edit-background-color);
    color: var(--ag-cell-batch-edit-text-color);
}

.ag-row-batch-edit {
    background-color: var(--ag-row-batch-edit-background-color);
    color: var(--ag-row-batch-edit-text-color);
}
```

